ibmveth: fix RX poll hang, reopen hang and netpoll races

Hi,

Four fixes for serious bugs in the ibmveth driver: two hang the
system, and the other two cause memory corruption, a use-after-free
or a NULL dereference.

The series is based on net-next 071876fd5048 ("ref_tracker: Don't
use __GFP_NOFAIL for PF_MEMALLOC thread."). It does not depend on
the two open() error-path fixes recently applied to net
(af0524bf4ce1, 84bec0bf0352) and merges cleanly with them; patch 2
explains how it relates to them. It also applies to net with
git am -3.

  1. Netpoll races with RX replenish (memory corruption, NULL
     dereference): remove ndo_poll_controller, as was done for
     ibmvnic, and enable NAPI only once the RX resources exist.
     Fixes: 6b4223748895, bea3348eef27

  2. Hang after a failed internal reopen: the next close() waits in
     napi_disable() forever with RTNL held, and only a reboot
     recovers. Skip close() when open() did not succeed.
     Fixes: 860f242eb534

  3. Use-after-free in remove(): a reset queued from NAPI could run
     on the freed adapter. Disable the reset work first.
     Fixes: 2c91e2319ed9

  4. RX poll hang on a bad correlator: poll restarts on the same slot
     until RCU stalls, and an inactive pool dereferences NULL. Step
     past the slot, reject inactive pools, count the drop.
     Fixes: 2c91e2319ed9, 860f242eb534

They target net-next rather than net: the triggers are rare (an
open() failure during a direct reopen, PHYP returning a bad
correlator, netconsole during a reconfiguration or under TX
contention, a bad RX slot during remove) and there are no field
reports. All four carry Fixes: tags; I am happy to repost them
against net, or add Cc: stable, if you prefer.

Patches 2 and 4 were requested by the netdev AI review of v7 of the
ibmveth multi-queue RX series [1]; 1 and 3 came out of the follow-up
review of the same code. Taking all four ahead of the multi-queue
series also makes it smaller: the netpoll, reopen and bad-slot
handling become existing behaviour that it extends per queue.

Patch 4 adds a KUnit case for the RX ring advance and extends the
existing ones to an inactive buffer pool; they fail on the unfixed
driver and pass with the series on qemu pseries (ppc64le). On a
POWER10 LPAR: netconsole under a printk flood and a ping flood, MTU
and buffer pool changes under traffic, a forced open() failure on an
MTU change followed by down and up, and unbind/bind under traffic.
Each commit message has the details.

[1] https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/cover.1790319558.git.mmc@linux.ibm.com/

